Exactly. And I’d widen it from “emotional range” to whole-system regulation.

The brain is not a set of isolated knobs, and the organs are not passive peripherals. You have interacting networks across cognition, movement, autonomic state, endocrine signaling, immune activity, interoception, respiration, heart rate, gut state, sleep, attention, and emotion. The useful skill is learning which signals belong to which layer and how strongly to modulate them.

So the instrument becomes:

\text{state}
=
\{
\text{attention},
\text{emotion},
\text{breath},
\text{posture},
\text{movement},
\text{arousal},
\text{interoception},
\text{thought},
\text{social context}
\}

Then tuning is not “make everything calm.” It is match the state to the task.

Competition may want higher arousal. Fine motor work may want steadiness. Grief may need room rather than suppression. Sleep needs down-regulation. Play may need spontaneity. Danger needs fast mobilization.

That gives you:

\boxed{\text{Regulation} \neq \text{suppression}}

Regulation means increasing, decreasing, redirecting, sustaining, or releasing a state deliberately within safe limits.

And the body gives constant feedback. Breath changes autonomic state. Posture changes perception and action-readiness. Movement changes arousal. Sleep changes cognition. Social contact can alter stress physiology. Attention itself changes which signals become amplified.

So you can train bidirectionally:

\text{mind}\rightarrow\text{body}

and

\text{body}\rightarrow\text{mind}

with feedback between them.

The boundary matters because physiology has hard constraints. You can train tolerance and control, but not every internal variable is safely or voluntarily controllable. The useful target is calibration, not domination.

In our language:

Learn the instrument deeply enough to know its range, its warning tones, its resonance, and when not to force the string.

Then expression becomes richer because you are not limited to one register. You can move across intensity, stillness, humor, aggression, tenderness, focus, recovery, and play without mistaking any one state for the whole self.